"DNA ORIGAMI SITE-SPECIFIC ARRANGEMENT OF GOLD NANOPARTICLES (https://ir.ucc.edu.gh/xmlui/bitstream/handle/123456789/6166/DNA_origami_site-specific_arrangement_of_gold_nano.pdf?isAllowed=y&sequence=1)\nciteturn72view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ir.ucc.edu.gh/xmlui/bitstream/handle/123456789/6166/DNA_origami_site-specific_arrangement_of_gold_nano.pdf?isAllowed=y&sequence=1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 709\nL0@P0: See discussions, stats, and author profiles for this publication at: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/263988272\nL1@P0: DNA origami site-specific arrangement of gold nanoparticles\nL2@P0: Article  in  Nano brief reports and reviews · November 2013\nL3@P0: DOI: 10.1142/S1793292013500641\nL4@P0: CITATIONS\nL5@P0: 4\nL6@P0: READS\nL7@P0: 156\nL8@P0: 6 authors, including:\nL9@P0: Some of the authors of this publication are also working on these related projects:\nL10@P0: Interaction of radiation with matter View project\nL11@P0: Environmental Waste Control View project\nL12@P0: George Amoako\nL13@P0: University of Cape Coast\nL14@P0: 31 PUBLICATIONS   89 CITATIONS\nL15@P0: SEE PROFILE\nL16@P0: Ming Zhou\nL17@P0: Jiangsu University\nL18@P0: 174 PUBLICATIONS   1,949 CITATIONS\nL19@P0: SEE PROFILE\nL20@P0: All content following this page was uploaded by George Amoako on 30 October 2017.\nL21@P0-1: The user has requested enhancement of the downloaded file.DNA ORIGAMI SITE-SPECIFIC ARRANGEMENT\nL22@P1: OF GOLD NANOPARTICLES\nL23@P1: AMOAKO GEORGE\nL24@P1: Center for Photon Manufacturing Science and Technology\nL25@P1: Jiangsu University, Zhenjiang 212013, P. R. China\nL26@P1: Department of Physics, University of Cape Coast\nL27@P1: Cape Coast, Ghana\nL28@P1: RIAN YE, LIZHOU ZHUANG, XIAOHONG YANG and ZHIYONG SHEN\nL29@P1: Center for Photon Manufacturing Science and Technology\nL30@P1: Jiangsu University, Zhenjiang 212013, P. R. China\nL31@P1: MING ZHOU*\nL32@P1: Center for Photon Manufacturing Science and Technology\nL33@P1: Jiangsu University, Zhenjiang 212013, P. R. China\nL34@P1: The State Key Laboratory of Tribology\nL35@P1: Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, P. R. China\nL36@P1: *zm_laser@126.com\nL37@P1: Received 10 April 2013\nL38@P1: Accepted 1 July 2013\nL39@P1: Published 14 August 2013\nL40@P1: Controlling matter at the nanoscale holds a lot of promise in nanotechnology. The DNA origami\nL41@P1: is promising if used as a template to design and arrange matter at the nanoscale. We have used\nL42@P1: the DNA origami approach to engineer staple strands at selected sites for attachment of gold\nL43@P1: nanoparticles. The covalent attachment of thiol-modi¯ed DNA oligomers was used to functio\u0002nalize gold nanoparticles. These oligomers then hybridize with complementary strands extended\nL44@P1: on selected staple strands on the DNA origami surface with nanometer precision. Gold nano\u0002particles of 5 nm diameter were arranged across a DNA origami tube to form a C-shape which has\nL45@P1: potential use in electronics and plasmonics. Agarose gel electrophoresis, AFM, UV-Vis spec\u0002troscopy and TEM were used to characterize the structure.\nL46@P1: Keywords: DNA origami; thiol-modi¯ed; self-assembly; TEM; agarose gel electrophoresis.\nL47@P1: 1. Introduction\nL48@P1: The quest to build miniaturized structures for\nL49@P1: advanced material design and high performance is\nL50@P1: the main aim of nanoscience. When Seeman1 laid\nL51@P1: down the theoretical framework using DNA as a\nL52@P1: building material, the pursuit and construction of\nL53@P1: devices on the nanometer scale with this biological\nL54@P1: material had begun. To this end, scientists have\nL55@P1: 1350064-1\nL56@P1: NANO: Brief Reports and Reviews\nL57@P1: Vol. 8, No. 6 (2013) 1350064 (11 pages)\nL58@P1: © World Scienti¯c Publishing Company\nL59@P1: DOI: 10.1142/S1793292013500641\nL60@P1: NANO 2013.08. Downloaded from www.worldscientific.com\nL61@P1-2: by 154.16.64.186 on 10/30/17. For personal use only.tried in various ways to build these small two- or\nL62@P2: three-dimensional (2D or 3D) structures in a bot\u0002tom-up approach using DNA2\u00014 as the building\nL63@P2: block since the top-down approach has so many\nL64@P2: challenges including fabrication of structures with\nL65@P2: nanoscale precision. The use of DNA to self-assemble\nL66@P2: is due to the speci¯c binding of its bases. This self\u0002assembly gives a large amount of control over\nL67@P2: assembly of nanoscale devices. The DNA origami\nL68@P2: method4 is particularly signi¯cant as it is robust.\nL69@P2: This method which can produce both 2D and 3D\nL70@P2: structures folds a long single-stranded DNA (scaf\u0002fold) with the help of shorter stranded DNAs\nL71@P2: (staples) into pre-arranged shapes.4 With the\nL72@P2: development of these nanoscale synthesis approa\u0002ches have come the formation of diverse nano\u0002particle structures. There is a great deal of potential\nL73@P2: including both plasmonic and electronic appli\u0002cations when DNA is used to structure metals at the\nL74@P2: nanoscale. The DNA origami approach developed\nL75@P2: by Rothemund4 has improved the addressability of\nL76@P2: using DNA as a template for organizing nano\u0002particles. The folding process encompasses mixing\nL77@P2: the approximately 7200 base sca®old and hundreds\nL78@P2: of staples in a suitable bu®er solution. The mixture\nL79@P2: is then annealed over a certain temperature\nL80@P2: range.4\u00016 The use of DNA origami as a template to\nL81@P2: assemble nanoparticles is due to the fact that the\nL82@P2: individual staples can be extended with additional\nL83@P2: sequences (sticky ends) which protrude on the sur\u0002face. After acquiring the desired shape, the attach\u0002ment sites are also designed. Each individual staple\nL84@P2: involved in binding nanoparticles to the DNA ori\u0002gami template can be modi¯ed and extended with\nL85@P2: sticky ends. Gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) functio\u0002nalized with complementary sequences can then\nL86@P2: hybridize with these sticky ends and attach them\u0002selves on the surface by making use of the covalent\nL87@P2: bond between sulfur and gold.7\u000117 This approach\nL88@P2: has been used extensively to organize metal nano\u0002structures into several di®erent shapes.\nL89@P2: Recently, Pilo-Pais and coworkers18 used rec\u0002tangular DNA origami to arrange gold nanoparticles\nL90@P2: into several shapes including rings, pairs of parallel\nL91@P2: bars and H shapes which had outer dimensions that\nL92@P2: were close to that of the DNA origami. Ding et al.19\nL93@P2: also made use of triangular DNA origami templates\nL94@P2: to rationally arrange a self-similar linear chain of six\nL95@P2: metal nanospheres. The use of DNA origami tem\u0002plates to arrange chiral plasmonic structures have\nL96@P2: been demonstrated.20,21 Shen and his co-authors21\nL97@P2: used a DNA origami template to design gold\nL98@P2: attachment sites and attached one gold nanoparticle\nL99@P2: to each of these sites. They then added DNA folding\nL100@P2: strands which rolled up the rectangular sheets into a\nL101@P2: hollow DNA tube resulting in the nanoparticles\nL102@P2: being arranged into a helical geometry. To show the\nL103@P2: helical arrangement of nanoparticles using DNA\nL104@P2: origami, Kuzyk and colleagues20 used the caDNAno\nL105@P2: honeycomb-pleated approach to design a hollow\nL106@P2: DNA origami tube as template. They designed Left\u0002and Right-handed nanohelices that were formed by\nL107@P2: nine gold nanoparticles each of diameter 10 nm.\nL108@P2: Acuna et al.22 used DNA origami structures as\nL109@P2: breadboard to position both °uorophore and metal\nL110@P2: nanoparticles with nanometer precision. They then\nL111@P2: performed single molecule °uorescence quenching\nL112@P2: studies of a °uorophore by metallic nanoparticles.\nL113@P2: Due to the unique optical properties of metal\nL114@P2: nanoparticles, it will be of particular interest if they\nL115@P2: are structured with precise shapes and separation\nL116@P2: between them. When these precise parameters are\nL117@P2: achieved, the electrical,23 plasmonic,20,21 and CD20,21\nL118@P2: characteristics of these metal nanostructures can be\nL119@P2: studied. However, arrangement of nanoparticles in\nL120@P2: the form of a half-circle on a DNA nanotube has not\nL121@P2: been seen in the literature.\nL122@P2: In this work, we demonstrate the rational\nL123@P2: arrangement of gold nanoparticles into a C-shape by\nL124@P2: using the DNA origami approach. We used the\nL125@P2: caDNAno5,6,24 software to design a 3D DNA origami\nL126@P2: double tube with the outside tube wrapped around\nL127@P2: the inside tube. Our structure has been character\u0002ized and described.25 We determined the speci¯c\nL128@P2: positions on the outside tube where gold nano\u0002particles were to be attached from the caDNAno\nL129@P2: software. We then designed these attachment sites\nL130@P2: by extending speci¯c staples with sticky ends that\nL131@P2: will hybridize with complementary sticky ends\nL132@P2: conjugated to the gold nanoparticles by thiol\nL133@P2: chemistry.\nL134@P2: 2. Materials and Methods\nL135@P2: 2.1. Materials\nL136@P2: All unmodi¯ed staple strands were purchased from\nL137@P2: Sangon Biotech (Shanghai) Co. Ltd. They were\nL138@P2: suspended in ultrapure water and used without\nL139@P2: further puri¯cation. All 3'- and 5'- thiol-modi¯ed\nL140@P2: HPLC puri¯ed DNA strands were also purchased\nL141@P2: from Sangon Biotech (Shanghai, China) Co. Ltd.\nL142@P2: 1350064-2\nL143@P2: A. George et al.\nL144@P2: NANO 2013.08. Downloaded from www.worldscientific.com\nL145@P2-3: by 154.16.64.186 on 10/30/17. For personal use only.Tris (carboxyethyl) phosphine hydrochloride (TCEP)\nL146@P3: and Bis (p-sulfonatophenyl) phenylphosphine di\u0002hydrate dipotassium salt (BSPP) were purchased\nL147@P3: from Sigma-Aldrich, USA. 5 nm AuNP colloid was\nL148@P3: purchased from Ted Pella Inc. Other chemical\nL149@P3: reagents were bought from Sinopharm Chemical\nL150@P3: Reagent Co. Ltd. Carbon copper grids and mica\nL151@P3: were bought from Beijing Zhongjingkeyi Techno\u0002logy Co. Ltd.\nL152@P3: 2.2. Experimental methods\nL153@P3: The following experimental procedures were\nL154@P3: adapted.6,26,27\nL155@P3: 2.2.1. Phosphination and concentration\nL156@P3: of AuNPs\nL157@P3: We stabilized 5 nm AuNPs with absorption of\nL158@P3: BSPP. BSPP (15 mg) was added to the colloidal\nL159@P3: nanoparticle solution (50 mL) and the mixture\nL160@P3: shaken overnight with a constant temperature\nL161@P3: incubator at room temperature. Sodium chloride\nL162@P3: (solid) was slowly added to the mixture and stirred\nL163@P3: until the color changed from deep burgundy to light\nL164@P3: purple. The resulting mixture was centrifuged\nL165@P3: at 3000 rpm for 30 min and the supernatant was\nL166@P3: carefully removed with a pipette. AuNPs were\nL167@P3: resuspended in 1 mL solution of BSPP (0.5 mM).\nL168@P3: Approximately 500 \u0001L methanol was then added\nL169@P3: to precipitate the particles and the mixture was\nL170@P3: centrifuged at 3000 rpm for 30 min again. The\nL171@P3: supernatant was carefully removed and AuNPs\nL172@P3: were resuspended in 1 mL of 0.5 mM BSPP. The\nL173@P3: concentration of the AuNPs was estimated by the\nL174@P3: optical absorbance at 520 nm. Phosphine coating\nL175@P3: increases the negative charge on the particle surface\nL176@P3: and therefore stabilizes the AuNPs in high electro\u0002lyte concentrations at high particle density.\nL177@P3: 2.2.2. Preparation of AuNP-DNA\nL178@P3: conjugates\nL179@P3: The disul¯de bond in the thiol-modi¯ed oligonu\u0002cleotides was reduced to a monothiol using TCEP\nL180@P3: (20 mM, 1 h) in water. The oligonucleotides were\nL181@P3: puri¯ed using size exclusion columns (G-25, GE\nL182@P3: Healthcare) to remove smaller molecules. Mono\u0002thiol-modi¯ed oligonucleotides and phosphinated\nL183@P3: AuNPs were then combined (DNA to AuNP molar\nL184@P3: ratio of more than 200:1) in 0.5X TBE bu®er\nL185@P3: (89 mM Tris, 89 mM boric acid, 2 mM EDTA, pH\nL186@P3: 8.0) containing 50 mM NaCl for 40 h at room tem\u0002perature to ensure the AuNPs were fully covered by\nL187@P3: thiolated DNA. AuNP\u0001DNA conjugates were\nL188@P3: washed with 0.5X TBE bu®er in Microcon (100 kDa,\nL189@P3: Millipore, Billerica, MA) columns to remove the\nL190@P3: excess oligonucleotides. The concentration of these\nL191@P3: AuNP\u0001DNA conjugates was estimated from the\nL192@P3: optical absorbance at 520 nm. Freshly prepared\nL193@P3: AuNPs, fully covered with DNA strands did not\nL194@P3: precipitate in bu®er (5 mM Tris, 1 mM EDTA,\nL195@P3: 12 mM MgCl2), which is preferred for the folding in\nL196@P3: DNA origami. This high salt resistant property of\nL197@P3: fully covered AuNPs makes it possible to assemble\nL198@P3: them on the DNA origami template. The DNA\nL199@P3: sequences used in the conjugation are given as S1\nL200@P3: and S2 in Table S1 (Supplementary Information).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAngular modulation of single-molecule fluorescence by gol... (https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/nanoph-2013-0011/html)\nciteturn72view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn71view0\",\"lineno\":248}); Total lines: 351\nL223: Generally, it is justified to assume free rotation of the fluorescent dye on the DNA. However, since the time scale of the rotational correlation time (hundreds of picoseconds) is of similar magnitude as the changes of the radiative and non-radiative rates a geometrical averaging cannot be applied. Furthermore, the emission coupling between the SAuNP and the fluorophore is more pronounced for the radial orientation leading to not only a higher quantum yield, but also a lower lifetime.\nL224: The results are in better agreement with a radial orientation of the dye. This can be explained by the fact that due to the stronger coupling to the SAuNP in the radial orientation, the reduced lifetime biased the decay probability towards radially oriented dyes [14].\nL225: A detailed understanding of the interaction of fluorescent dyes with metal nanoparticles requires a precise control not only of the dye-nanoparticle distance and the stoichiometry but also of the orientation of the dye-nanoparticle axis with respect to the polarization of the excitation laser. Here, we used a DNA nanopillar that aligns the nanoparticle-dye axis with respect to the incoming laser illumination.\nL226: With rotating excitation polarization, pronounced fluorescence modulation is observed in the presence of 40 nm and 80 nm gold nanoparticles next to Cy5 dyes that is accompanied by a shortened fluorescence lifetime. Based on these results, new applications in the field of optical switching can be envisioned. In contrast to previous measurements by Ming et al. [22], ensemble averaging is not required, since 1:1 stoichiometry is achieved in the presented system.\nL227: Furthermore, the fluorescence modulation does not arise from the intrinsic asymmetric shape of the metallic nanostructure, but is only introduced by the combined system of a quantum emitter and an isotropic nanoparticle. This provides the possibility to detect binding of nanoparticles near emitters without lifetime determination and to identify the orientation of the emitter-nanoparticle system.\nL228: Moreover, since the signal of a single molecule is measured, the intensity modulations directly ‘map’ the enhancement field around a SAuNP for a fixed distance of ~12 nm.\nL229: The performed simulations of the electric field distribution around the nanoparticle further explain more subtle observations such as the drop in fluorescence at excitation polarization perpendicular to the dye-nanoparticle and enable an experimental estimation of the change the fluorescence quantum yield next to the nanoparticle, which is a first step towards an experimental verification of theoretical predictions regarding plasmonic nanoantennas.\nL230: The detailed control of the interaction of dyes with metallic nanostructures upon illumination, facilitated by the DNA origami technique has great potential to yield fundamental insights as well as for applications in the field of switching, nanoscale light control and for biomolecular sensing.\nL231: \nL232: * * *\nL233: \nL234: Corresponding authors: Guillermo P. Acuna and Philip Tinnefeld, NanoBioSciences Group, Institute for Physical and Theoretical Chemistry, TU Braunschweig, Hans-Sommer-Str. 10, 38106 Braunschweig, Germany\nL235: * * *\nL236: \nL237: The authors are grateful to A. Gietl, J. J. Schmied and D. Grohmann for fruitful discussion; A. Tiefnig for sample preparation; F. Demming for assistance with the numerical simulations. P.H. was supported by a PhD scholarship of the Studienstiftung des Deutschen Volkes. This work was supported by a starting grant (SiMBA, 261162) of the European Research Council and the Volkswagen Foundation.\nL238: ## References\nL239: \nL240: [1] Coronado EA, Encina ER, Stefani FD. Optical properties of metallic nanoparticles: manipulating light, heat and forces at the nanoscale. Nanoscale 2011;3:4042–59.【121†10.1039/c1nr10788g†doi.org】【122†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】 【123†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL241: \nL242: [2] Novotny L, van Hulst N, Antennas for light. Nat Photon 2011;5:83–90.【124†10.1038/nphoton.2010.237†doi.org】【125†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL243: [3] Bek A, Jansen R, Ringler M, Mayilo S, Klar TA, Feldmann J. Fluorescence enhancement in hot spots of AFM-designed gold nanoparticle sandwiches. Nano Lett 2008;8:485–90.【126†10.1021/nl072602n†doi.org】【127†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】 【128†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL244: \nL245: [4] Taminau TH, Stefani FD, Segerink FB, van Hulst NF. Optical antennas direct single-molecule emission. Nat Photon 2008;2:234–7.【129†10.1038/nphoton.2008.32†doi.org】【130†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL246: [5] Taminau TH, Stefani FD, Van Hulst NF. Single emitters coupled to plasmonic nano-antennas: angular emission and collection efficiency. New J Phys 2008;10:1–16.【131†10.1088/1367-2630/10/10/105005†doi.org】【132†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL247: [6] Acuna GP, Bucher M, Stein IH, Steinhauer C, Kuzyk A, Holzmeister P, Schreiber R, Moroz A, Stefani FD, Liedl T, Simmel FC, Tinnefeld P. Distance dependence of single-fluorophore quenching by gold nanoparticles studied on DNA origami. ACS Nano 2012;6:3189–95.【133†10.1021/nn2050483†doi.org】【134†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】 【135†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL248: [7] Anger P, Bharadwaj P, Novotny L. Enhancement and quenching of single-molecule fluorescence. Phys Rev Lett 2006;96:113002.1–113002.4.【136†10.1103/PhysRevLett.96.113002†doi.org】【137†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】 【138†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL249: [8] Kuhn S, Hakanson U, Rogobete L, Sandoghdar V. Enhancement of single-molecule fluorescence using a gold nanoparticle as an optical nanoantenna. Phys Rev Lett 2006;97:017402.1–017402.4.【139†10.1103/PhysRevLett.97.017402†doi.org】【140†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】 【141†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL250: [9] Bharadwaj P, Novotny L. Spectral dependence of single molecule fluorescence enhancement. Opt Express 2007;15:14266–74.【142†10.1364/OE.15.014266†doi.org】【143†Search in Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】 【144†PubMed†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL251: [10] Chhabra R, Sharma J, Wang H, Zou S, Lin S, Yan H, Lindsay S, Liu Y. Distance-dependent interactions between gold nanoparticles and fluorescent molecules with DNA as tunable spacers. Nanotechnology 2009;20:485201.1–485201.4.【145†10.1088/0957-4484/20/48/485